

##### Why do we need to allign our reads to a reference genome? #####

In your ddRAD workflow, the alignment step is done to determine where each sequencing read came from in the genome.
Your sequencer gives you millions of short DNA fragments (reads), but initially they are just isolated sequences with no positional information. 
By aligning them to a reference genome from a related bat species, you can place those reads onto approximate genomic locations.
  
Why alignment is necessary in ddRAD population genetics
Suppose one of your reads is:
ATCGGATCCTAGGCTA

By itself, this sequence is not very useful because you do not know:
which chromosome it belongs to
which genomic region it came from
whether another individual has the same locus
whether differences are true variants or sequencing errors

Alignment solves this.
The aligner (like BWA or Bowtie2) compares each read against the reference genome and finds the best matching location.
After alignment, the read becomes something like:
Chromosome 5 : position 12,543,221
Now reads from all individuals can be compared at the same genomic coordinates.

## Why this is especially important for ddRAD ##

ddRAD sequences only a subset of the genome near restriction enzyme cut sites.
Without alignment:
loci from different individuals cannot easily be matched
SNP calling becomes difficult
population analyses become unreliable

Alignment allows you to:
identify homologous loci across individuals
detect SNPs
calculate allele frequencies
estimate heterozygosity
perform PCA/admixture/FST analyses
infer phylogeny and population structure

## Why use a related species genome for alligning the reads? ##

You usually do not have a reference genome for your exact species.
Using a genome from the same genus works because closely related species still share large portions of their DNA sequence.
The aligner tolerates some mismatches caused by evolutionary divergence.

However:
if the reference is too distant, alignment quality decreases
some reads may fail to map
bias can occur in variant calling
For species within the same genus, this is usually acceptable for population genomics.

##### What is a SAM file? ####
  
SAM = Sequence Alignment/Map format.
It is a text file that stores:
the original sequencing read
where it aligned in the reference genome
alignment quality information
mismatch information

It is essentially a record of how every read mapped to the genome.
  
Simple analogy
Imagine:
reads = puzzle pieces
reference genome = picture on the puzzle box
Alignment tries to place every puzzle piece in the correct location on the picture.
The SAM file is the report describing where every piece was placed and how well it fit.
  
## Structure of a SAM file ##

A SAM file has:
1. Header lines
These start with @
Example: @SQ SN:chr1 LN:248956422
This describes the reference sequence.
  
2. Alignment lines
Each line represents one sequencing read.
Example:
READ001  0  chr1  1050  60  100M  *  0  0  ACTG...  IIII...

Important SAM columns
Column	  Meaning
QNAME	   Read name
FLAG	   Information about mapping
RNAME	   Reference chromosome/scaffold
POS	      Alignment start position
MAPQ	   Mapping quality
CIGAR	   How the read aligned
SEQ	    Read sequence
QUAL  	Base quality scores

## Example explained ##
READ001  0  chr1  1050  60  100M
Means:
Read name = READ001
aligned to chromosome 1
starting at position 1050
mapping quality = 60 (very confident)
100M = 100 matched bases

## What the CIGAR string means ##

The CIGAR string describes alignment operations.
Example: 90M2I8M
Means:
90 matched bases
2 inserted bases
8 matched bases

## Other symbols: ##
Symbol   	Meaning
M	       Match/mismatch
I	       Insertion
D	       Deletion
S      	Soft clipping
H	      Hard clipping

In a SAM/BAM CIGAR string, hard clipping (H) and soft clipping (S) both mean that part of a sequencing read did not align to the reference genome.
The difference is whether the clipped bases are still kept in the SAM/BAM record.

Soft clipping (S)
Soft-clipped bases are:
NOT aligned to the reference
but are still stored in the read sequence

Example: 10S90M
Means:
first 10 bases were clipped
next 90 bases aligned

Hard clipping (H)
Hard-clipped bases are:
NOT aligned AND completely removed from the stored sequence

Example: 10H90M
Means:
first 10 bases were discarded entirely only the remaining 90 aligned bases are stored
The clipped bases do not appear in the SAM/BAM sequence field.

