#### What is a BAM file and why  do we need to convert SAM to BAM ####
  
A BAM file is the binary compressed version of a SAM file.
SAM = human-readable text format
BAM = compact binary format
Both contain the same alignment information, but BAM is much more efficient for computational work.

Why SAM files are problematic
SAM files are huge because they are plain text.
For example:
a SAM file from ddRAD data may be several GB
whole genome sequencing SAM files can exceed hundreds of GB

Every read is written as readable text like:
READ001  0  chr1  1050  60  100M ...
Text storage is inefficient.

Main reasons we convert SAM → BAM
1. Smaller file size
BAM files are usually much smaller.

Example:
File type	Approximate size
SAM	  40 GB
BAM	  5–10 GB
This saves:
storage space
transfer time
RAM usage

2. Faster computation
Programs process BAM files faster because:
compressed binary reading is efficient
random access is possible
parsing text is avoided
Tools like:
bcftools, samtools, GATK, Qualimap are optimized for BAM.

3. BAM files can be indexed
This is extremely important.
An index (.bai) allows software to jump directly to specific genomic regions.
Without indexing:
Find reads on chromosome 5 at position 1,000,000
for this the program would need to scan the entire file.
With indexing:
it jumps directly to the needed region instantly
This enables:
genome visualization in IGV, rapid SNP calling, efficient coverage analysis

Typical workflow
You usually do: SAM ↓ BAM ↓ sorted BAM ↓ indexed BAM

## What “sorted BAM” means ##
Reads are arranged by genomic coordinates.
Example:
chr1:100
chr1:150
chr1:220
chr2:50

Sorting is required before indexing and variant calling.

## Why reads are initially unsorted ##
During alignment, reads are processed in the order they appear in the FASTQ file.
So the BAM file may look like:
chr2:300
chr1:100
chr5:900
chr1:220
chr2:80
This random order is inefficient for downstream analyses.

## Why sorting is necessary ##
  
1. Variant calling requires grouped reads
To identify SNPs, programs must examine all reads covering the same genomic position.

Example:
At position chr1:1000, the variant caller needs all overlapping reads together:
Read1 → A
Read2 → A
Read3 → G
Read4 → A
If reads are scattered randomly throughout the BAM file, the program would constantly search the entire file.
Sorting places nearby genomic reads together, making this efficient.

2. BAM indexing only works on sorted BAMs
When you create a BAM index (.bai), the software records:
“reads for this genomic region are located here in the file”
This only works if reads are ordered by coordinates.
Without sorting, indexing becomes impossible or meaningless.

