## Indexing a BAM file ##
Indexing a BAM file means creating a separate lookup file that allows software to quickly locate reads from specific genomic regions without 
scanning the entire BAM file.

Why indexing is needed
A BAM file may contain millions of aligned reads.
Suppose you want to examine: chr5:1,000,000–1,001,000
Without an index:
the software must read the entire BAM file from beginning to end. This is very slow
With an index:
the software jumps directly to the relevant part of the BAM file.
This makes genomic analysis fast.

## Why BAM must be sorted before indexing ##
Indexing only works properly if reads are arranged by genomic position.

Example of sorted BAM:
chr1:100
chr1:150
chr1:220
chr1:400
chr2:50
Because reads are ordered, the index can record:
chr1 positions 1–100000 → located near byte X in file
chr1 positions 100001–200000 → located near byte Y
If reads were random, this would not work.

## What the index actually stores ##
The .bai file does NOT store the reads themselves.

Instead, it stores pointers telling software approximately:
where genomic regions are located inside the BAM file.
file offsets (byte positions).

So when a tool requests:
chr3:500000
the index says:
“start reading the BAM file around byte position XXXXX”
This avoids reading the whole file.

## What happens internally ##

Step 1: Read sorted BAM
The indexing program scans the BAM file sequentially.

Step 2: Divide genome into regions (“bins”)
The genome is split into genomic intervals.

Example:
chr1:
1–16384
16385–32768
32769–49152
...
  
Step 3: Record file locations
For each genomic interval, the index stores:
genomic region → BAM file position

## Important point ##
The BAM index is tied to that exact BAM file.
If you modify the BAM file (sorting, filtering, etc.), you must regenerate the index.
Otherwise:
coordinates no longer match
tools may fail or give errors


  




  
  













  




